1. A BARE %x EATS FOUR BYTES, NOT ONE

$ hexdump -e '"%x "' eight.bin; echo
64636261 68676665 

$ hexdump -e '/1 "%x "' eight.bin; echo
61 62 63 64 65 66 67 68 
   The man page's table: %d %i %o %u %X %x default to a FOUR-byte unit, and
   1, 2 and 4 are the only sizes allowed. 64636261 is 'abcd' read as one
   little-endian 32-bit number — the bare-hexdump swap, twice as wide. Write
   the byte count every time: /1 for bytes.

2. THE INPUT RUNS OUT MID-FORMAT: THE BLOCK IS ZERO-PADDED

$ hexdump -e '2/2 "%04x " "\n"' three.bin
6261 0063
   Three bytes, a format that wants four. The unit that OVERLAPS the end
   gets 63 plus a zero byte and prints 0063: the padding is real and it is
   inside a number you might read as data. There is no 00 in the file.

3. UNITS ENTIRELY PAST THE END BECOME SPACES, AND THE LAST ITERATION LOSES ITS TRAILING SPACE

$ hexdump -e '4/2 "%04x " "|\n"' one.bin | cat -vet
0061               |$

$ hexdump -e '4/2 "%04x " "|\n"' eight.bin | cat -vet
6261 6463 6665 6867|$
   One byte, four two-byte units. Unit 1 overlaps the end and prints 0061;
   units 2 to 4 are past it and print as many spaces as they would have
   printed characters. With a full block every unit prints, and the space
   after the LAST one is dropped — which is why a full line ends '6867|'
   and the short one is exactly as wide. This is where the trailing spaces
   on every dump tool's short last line come from.

4. THE BLOCK IS THE LONGEST FORMAT STRING, AND A SHORTER ONE GROWS TO FIT

$ hexdump -e '8/1 "%02x " "\n"' -e '"%_p"' -e '"\n"' eight.bin
61 62 63 64 65 66 67 68
abcdefgh

$ hexdump -e '8/1 "%02x " "\n"' -e '"%_p" "\n"' eight.bin
61 62 63 64 65 66 67 68
a
   The block is eight bytes because the first string wants eight. The second
   string asked for ONE %_p with no count, and the man page's rule is exact:
   a string whose LAST unit consumes bytes and has no iteration count has
   that count raised until the block is used up — so it printed all eight.
   Move the "\n" into the same string and %_p is no longer the last unit:
   the rule does not fire, one character prints, and seven bytes go unshown.
   Every -e string sees the same block from its start either way.

5. %_u NAMES THE CONTROL CHARACTERS

$ hexdump -e '8/1 "%_u " "\n"' ctl.bin | cat -vet
a ht b lf nul del  $

$ hexdump -e '8/1 "%_c " "\n"' ctl.bin | cat -vet
a \t b \n \0 177  $

$ hexdump -e '8/1 "%_p " "\n"' ctl.bin | cat -vet
a . b . . .  $
   Three ways to draw a byte you cannot draw. %_u prints the ASCII control
   name in lower case (ht, lf, nul, del); %_c prints the C escape where one
   exists (\t, \n, \0) and three-digit octal otherwise (177 for DEL);
   %_p gives up with a dot. Two units were past the end: spaces again.

6. -f: THE FORMAT STRINGS IN A FILE, ONE PER LINE, # FOR A COMMENT

$ hexdump -f perusal.fmt s.txt
000000  63 61 66 c3 a9 3a 20 31  e2 82 ac 0a              |caf..: 1....|
   -e strings and -f files are applied in the order given, and a format
   file is the one place a layout you use every day can live.

7. THE OFFSET IS CUMULATIVE ACROSS FILES

$ hexdump -C three.bin eight.bin
00000000  61 62 63 61 62 63 64 65  66 67 68                 |abcabcdefgh|
0000000b
   Two files, one dump: eight.bin's first byte is at offset 3. hexdump is
   a filter over the concatenation, which is exactly what cat a b | hexdump
   would have shown — the file boundary is not in the output anywhere.

8. -s TAKES 0x, AND -n COUNTS FROM THERE

$ hexdump -C -s 0x8 -n 4 s.txt
00000008  e2 82 ac 0a                                       |....|
0000000c
   A leading 0x makes the offset hexadecimal, a leading 0 makes it octal,
   plain digits are decimal — the C rules. The offset column still shows
   the position in the file, not in the window.

9. EXIT STATUS: 0, AND >0 IF A FILE CANNOT BE READ
   hexdump -C s.txt          -> 0
   hexdump -C no_such_file   -> 1  (message on stderr, not shown)
